<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lebanon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lebanon is a mountainous country on the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast, historically famous for its cedar forests. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> drew on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> of Lebanon for its building projects. Shia scholars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-amil">Jabal Amil</a> in southern Lebanon were invited to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>, where they helped establish Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as the state religion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lebanon.jpg" alt="Lebanon" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henri Pharaon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Lebanon.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lebanon lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> between latitudes 33° and 35° N, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> to the north and east, Israel to the south and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean Sea</a> to the west. Its area is 10,452 square kilometres. The country has four physiographic regions: a narrow coastal plain, the Lebanon mountain range, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa Valley</a> and the Anti-Lebanon Mountains. The Lebanon range peaks at 3,088 metres in Qurnat as Sawda', while the Beqaa, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-rift">Great Rift Valley</a> system, is 180 kilometres long. The longest river, the Leontes, is 145 kilometres long and empties into the Mediterranean north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>, among the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world, shows settlement earlier than 5000 BCE. Lebanon was part of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> and the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a>, whose most prominent cities were Byblos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>. The Phoenicians are credited with the oldest verified alphabet.</p><p>Foreign powers, beginning with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a>, imposed tribute; the cities passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a> and in 539 BCE to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> after the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> in 332 BCE. Pompey annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 64 BCE. In the late 4th and early 5th century a hermit named Maron founded a monastic tradition whose followers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maronites">Maronites</a>, moved into the mountains.</p><p>After the seventh century the region came under Arab caliphates, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>, and in the early 16th century Ottoman rule. The Mount Lebanon Mutasarrifate was set up under Sultan Abdulmejid I as a home for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maronites">Maronite Christians</a>. France administered the Mandate for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the Lebanon after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and created Greater Lebanon in 1920; independence came by 1943. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanese-civil-war">Lebanese Civil War</a> lasted from 1975 to 1990, and Syria occupied the country from 1976 to 2005 and Israel from 1982 to 2000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-pact">National Pact</a> of 1943 apportions power among the religious groups: the president is a Maronite Christian, the prime minister a Sunni Muslim and the speaker of Parliament a Shi'a Muslim. The 128 seats of Parliament are divided equally between Christians and Muslims, a balance fixed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif-agreement">Taif Agreement</a> that ended the civil war. No official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> has been held since 1932 because of the sensitivity of the confessional balance. More Lebanese, counting partial ancestry, live abroad than in the country, with the largest community in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lebanon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Holy See of Cilicia survives at Antelias in <b>Lebanon</b>, and the lion, emblem of the Cilician state, stands on the coat of arms of Armenia today.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Second World War about 150,000 Armenians from countries such as Cyprus, France, Greece, Iraq, <b>Lebanon</b> and Syria came between 1946 and 1948.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>At its peak the Neo-Assyrian economy channelled the agricultural surpluses of Mesopotamia, the timber of <b>Lebanon</b>, the metals of Anatolia, the horses of the Zagros and the luxury goods of Phoenician trade into the construction of enormous royal cities at Nimrud, Khorsabad and Nineveh.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Byblos, called Gubla by its own people and in cuneiform texts, Gebal in the Hebrew Bible and Jbeil today, lies on a low headland on the coast of <b>Lebanon</b> about 35 kilometres north of Beirut.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the First World War, France regained Alsace–Lorraine, occupied the Saar Basin, shared Germany's African colonies such as Kamerun with Britain and took the Mandate of Syria and the Mandate of <b>Lebanon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>He defeated the Hasmonean Jews, and at its zenith his realm stretched over parts of the Caucasus and what is now south-eastern Turkey, Iran, Syria and <b>Lebanon</b>, one of the most powerful states of the Roman East.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Military</a></li></ul></section>
